CHROMIUM: bcmdhd: fix 2.4 GHz HT40 channel flags to stop periodic
reg-enforcement disconnects

Connections to 40 MHz-wide 2.4 GHz APs drop every ~70 s with

	[dhd] [wlan0] wl_cfg80211_disconnect : Reason 3, act 1, ...

The disconnect comes from cfg80211's regulatory enforcement worker
(reg_check_chans_work -> cfg80211_leave, confirmed via the
cfg80211:rdev_disconnect tracepoint): every association files a
regulatory hint (country IE), which arms the worker with its 60 s
grace period; the worker then fetches the live chandef from the
driver (channel 11, width 40 MHz) and rejects it against the wiphy
channel flags, because every 2.4 GHz channel is registered with
IEEE80211_CHAN_NO_HT40MINUS|PLUS. Firmware associates at 40 MHz
regardless, so the link is torn down and the cycle repeats after
reconnect.

The wrong flags come from wl_construct_reginfo(): the 2 GHz
"ht40_allowed = WL_BW_CAP_40MHZ(bw_cap_2g)" assignment sits inside
"#ifdef EXT_REGD_INFO", which this build never defines (the only
Makefile reference is commented out and misspelled EXT_REG_INFO),
while the 5 GHz/6 GHz assignments are unconditional. ht40_allowed
therefore stays false for the whole 2 GHz pass: the 40 MHz chanspecs
that would clear the NO_HT40 flags are skipped, and every 20 MHz
entry gets flags = IEEE80211_CHAN_NO_HT40 - contradicting both the
firmware bw_cap and the band's own HT caps (HT20/HT40).

Make the 2 GHz assignment unconditional, matching the 5 GHz/6 GHz
handling and older bcmdhd releases.

Index: kernel-rockchip/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rkwifi/bcmdhd/wl_cfg80211.c
===================================================================
--- kernel-rockchip.orig/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rkwifi/bcmdhd/wl_cfg80211.c
+++ kernel-rockchip/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rkwifi/bcmdhd/wl_cfg80211.c
@@ -19395,9 +19395,7 @@ static int wl_construct_reginfo(struct b
 			(channel <= CH_MAX_2G_CHANNEL)) {
 			band_chan_arr = __wl_2ghz_channels;
 			array_size = ARRAYSIZE(__wl_2ghz_channels);
-#ifdef EXT_REGD_INFO
 			ht40_allowed = WL_BW_CAP_40MHZ(bw_cap_2g);
-#endif /* EXT_REGD_INFO */
 		}
 #ifdef CFG80211_6G_SUPPORT
 		else if (CHSPEC_IS6G(chspec) && (channel >= CH_MIN_6G_CHANNEL) &&
